  "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n13,\\,8,\\,3,\\,-2,\\,…\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is ${a}_{1}=13$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=8-13=-5.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =13+(n-1)(-5) \\\\  & =13-5n+5 \\\\  & =18-5n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $33$rd term. All we have to do is substitute $n=33:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =18-5n \\\\ {a}_{33} & =18-5(33) \\\\  & =18-165 \\\\  & =-147\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $33$rd term of the sequence is ${a}_{33}=-147.$",
